WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:13.390
So now I'd like to
introduce today's speaker.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:16.170
Today's featured speaker is Olav Solgaard.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.950
Olav Solgaard is the professor
of electrical engineering

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.910
of Stanford University, and the director
of the Edward L Ginzton Laboratory.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:27.438
He earned his PhD degree from Stanford
University in 1992, where his doctoral

00:00:27.438 --> 00:00:31.578
dissertation was the basis for the
establishment of a Silicon Valley firm,

00:00:31.578 --> 00:00:36.920
Silicon Light Machines, also known as SLM,
cofounded by Dr. Solgaard in 1994.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.650
He was a postdoc at UC Berkley,
and in 1995 he joined

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:44.610
the electrical engineering faculty of
the University of California Davis.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.550
His work at UC Davis lead to the invention
of the multiwavelength fiber optical

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:52.790
switch, which has been developed into
commercial products by several companies.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:55.995
Professor Solgaard's research
interests include optical MEMS,

00:00:55.995 --> 00:01:01.140
photonic crystals, atomic force
microscopy, and solar energy conversions.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:05.360
He has authored more than 300 technical
publications and holds 50 patents.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.380
He is a fellow of The Optical Society of
America, the Royal Norwegian Society of

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:14.700
Sciences and Letters, and the Norwegian
Academy of Technological Sciences.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.636
He also serves as the academic director
for the new Internet of Things graduate

00:01:18.636 --> 00:01:21.900
certificate offered through the Stanford
Center for Professional Development,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.570
which we will talk a little bit
more about at the end of the hour.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.650
So now I'd like to turn
the floor over to Olav.

00:01:30.831 --> 00:01:34.991
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much, Stephanie, and
thank you to all of you out there who have

00:01:34.991 --> 00:01:38.640
logged on to hear me talk about things
that are very dear to my heart.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.430
I've been working with sensors for a long
period, a long time, so smart sensors

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:47.740
are something that I'm very excited
about and have been for a long time.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:51.634
The Internet of Things, this newer base
also has changed how we look at sensors

00:01:51.634 --> 00:01:55.235
quite a bit, and that's part of what
I'm going to be talking about today.

00:01:55.235 --> 00:02:02.780
So here's my outline, I'm going to talk
a little bit about sensor fundamentals.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.320
Because that allows you to sort of
understand a little bit better how you

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:12.820
should design and operate sensors in the
Internet of Things sort of environment.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:18.830
And I will make the argument, and
I hope I make a strong argument that MEMS

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:23.460
sensors, meaning sensors that are made
with silicon foundary technologies.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.534
And I'll talk a little
bit about what they are.

00:02:25.534 --> 00:02:30.438
All the ones that really are going to
enable the Internet, and has been doing so

00:02:30.438 --> 00:02:34.100
up till now, and will do it even
more on that in the future.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:39.158
And then I will, towards the end, once
we have sort of understood what a MEMS

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:44.376
sensor is and what it does, I'll talk
a little bit about Internet of Things and

00:02:44.376 --> 00:02:47.560
how that is going to be
enabled by MEMS sensors.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.263
And I'll talk a little bit about
the opportunities that I see.

00:02:51.263 --> 00:02:57.618
And really inviting you to sort of think
about what Internet of Things can be for

00:02:57.618 --> 00:03:01.548
you in terms of challenges and
opportunities.

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:06.693
So that's towards the end, but before I
even jump into the center part of it,

00:03:06.693 --> 00:03:11.705
I just wanted to say a few things about
my perception on Internet of things.

00:03:11.705 --> 00:03:15.937
And Internet of Things is
showing here as a pink cloud,

00:03:15.937 --> 00:03:19.070
has many components to it, obviously.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:22.424
It is sensors, or
I have the perspective of sensors,

00:03:22.424 --> 00:03:26.460
and many other people look at
tit from different perspectives.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.290
So you might be an expert on the Internet
and how you use the Internet.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.440
Of course that's really the backbone
that ties everything together.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:38.360
Machine learning and signal processing
at the far end is also going to be very

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.984
important, and many other opportunities
and challenges are there.

00:03:41.984 --> 00:03:46.814
Circuits and embedded systems are the
things that sort of takes the sensors and

00:03:46.814 --> 00:03:48.800
connects them to the Internet.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.676
So you can think of it in this way,

00:03:50.676 --> 00:03:55.110
the sensors are the structures that allows
us to interact with the real world.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:59.206
And then they take it all the way to the
other end through the embedded systems,

00:03:59.206 --> 00:04:03.367
through the circuits, through the
Internet, through machine learning, and

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:05.870
signal processing into the digital world.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.870
So the sensors are the interface,
if you like.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:11.013
And that's why I think they
are the most important,

00:04:11.013 --> 00:04:13.560
of course other people have
different perspectives on that.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.690
But here's some of the reasons why
I think sensors are so important.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.136
You use them just everyday,
this is two very,

00:04:21.136 --> 00:04:24.589
very incomplete lists that sort of
sensors that you use every day,

00:04:24.589 --> 00:04:27.816
involved with sensors are in
your cellphone in particular.

00:04:27.816 --> 00:04:32.276
In that you have a accelerometers in
there, those are the ones that allows your

00:04:32.276 --> 00:04:35.870
screen to rotate so
the phone knows what orientation it is.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:39.830
Gyros do some of the same things,
also complements your GPS signals.

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:44.804
Timing circuits, microphones,
light sensors, cameras, all these things,

00:04:44.804 --> 00:04:47.695
heartbeat sensors, and proximity sensors.

00:04:47.695 --> 00:04:50.000
The heartbeat sensor is the one
that's fits in your Fitbit or

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.880
sort of device like that, that people
more and more are using these days.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.750
In your car you have many,
many dozens of sensors, and

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:00.870
I'm sort of showing
an incomplete list here.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:05.970
The next thing I wanted to do was
to talk about how sensors work.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.845
And of course,
if you look at this list, and

00:05:07.845 --> 00:05:11.043
this is a partial list, you'll say,
that's not a meaningful question,

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:13.360
because they obviously work
in many different ways.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.314
And so
I'll try to find some commonalities so

00:05:16.314 --> 00:05:21.770
I can say a few things about how sensors
should work and how they should scale.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.360
So let's take a look at some of
the ones that are out there.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.450
This is Standford work,
this is a company called Si Time.

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:30.700
What they do is make clocks, essentially.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.560
So these are clocks that
are buried in your silicon chip.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:38.439
And timing is, of course, super important
when you do communications, obviously.

00:05:38.439 --> 00:05:40.970
This is the type of stuff that
sits behind everything you do.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:44.250
You use it all the time,
you don't even know it.

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:48.300
The really cool things about
this particular clock,

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.750
if you like, is it work exactly like
your grandfather clock at home.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.600
It has an oscillator and

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.340
the frequency of that oscillation is
what gives you the timing information.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:03.046
Only this is a much more advanced
thing and much more stable thing and

00:06:03.046 --> 00:06:06.700
much better in terms of keeping
time than your grandfather clock.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:12.576
What we have here is,
if you look at this piece that is sort of,

00:06:12.576 --> 00:06:16.370
you can see as a cross section here.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.050
That is a mass that is
suspended on a spring.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.091
And it's driven and resonance frequency,

00:06:21.091 --> 00:06:24.900
we'll talk a little bit more about
a resonance frequencies in a second.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.330
But that needs to be very,
very, well defined so

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:30.340
that you can get a very good time signal.

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:32.930
So this is a mass on
the spring essentially.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:37.664
Here's another example,
this is a MEMS gyroscope.

00:06:37.664 --> 00:06:40.761
There are several things
I want to say about this,

00:06:40.761 --> 00:06:42.910
this is a very complex structure.

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:45.760
The first I showed was from Stanford,
this is from UC Berkeley.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.035
I don't want to be too Stanford-centric,

00:06:48.035 --> 00:06:52.160
although you might feel that I am
already when I go through these sides.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:56.360
This is from Berkeley, and
the part that's the sensor is the gyro.

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:59.690
And so
this is something that senses rotation.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:05.330
So it's similar to the timing circuit
I just shown in the sense that it's

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:09.660
essentially a mass suspended on a spring.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.684
And that's the center of it, and

00:07:11.684 --> 00:07:15.020
there is also a whole lot
more going on here obviously.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:19.160
Here's some of my work.this is
an atomic force microscope.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:23.561
So this allows us to send things
down to the nanometer scale, and

00:07:23.561 --> 00:07:29.367
maybe as little as 1 nanometer chip,
more chip you get 10 nanometer resolution.

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:34.108
I show two different versions here,
both of them have this same thing.

00:07:34.108 --> 00:07:38.877
There is, if you look, there is a spring
and there's a mass associated with

00:07:38.877 --> 00:07:43.738
that spring, sort of at the end of
the spring, and that allows us to vibrate.

00:07:43.738 --> 00:07:48.374
If you look at the one the left, it sort
of has a vibrating type of structure,

00:07:48.374 --> 00:07:50.630
like a cantilever or a springboard.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:55.590
The one on the right is more like
a trampoline or something like that.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:59.560
In both cases you have a spring and
you have a mass suspended from it, and

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:05.517
that's part of the important operational
characteristics of these devices.

00:08:05.517 --> 00:08:06.350
Here's another one.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:10.520
This is not really a sensor, but it's the
type of stuff that could go into a LIDAR,

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:11.060
for instance.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:15.540
This is a scanner,
you see there's a mirror right there, and

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:20.552
that mirror is rotating, again,
it's a rotating mass on a spring.

00:08:20.552 --> 00:08:25.503
This is they will set in
future LIDAR systems.

00:08:25.503 --> 00:08:28.220
And we're really trying to miniaturize
those to make them more efficient.

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:33.950
So LIDAR is the ranging system, an imaging
system they use in self-driving cars.

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:41.384
Maybe the most famous example is the
Texas Instruments projection technology,

00:08:41.384 --> 00:08:45.468
they call it
Digital Light Processing Technology.

00:08:45.468 --> 00:08:48.111
But that is just a little
aluminum mirrors, and

00:08:48.111 --> 00:08:52.549
one of them in the center is ripped off
here to show you the sort of substructure.

00:08:52.549 --> 00:08:54.520
And that substructure is very interesting.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:58.402
But again, it's a spring that sort of
you can see those little white pieces.

00:08:58.402 --> 00:09:01.493
Those are springs that connect
it through the mirror, and

00:09:01.493 --> 00:09:03.560
the mirror rotates on the spring.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.200
Underneath there's all
kinds of of other things.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:08.980
There is integrated electronics
in this particular chip,

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:14.290
and that's a theme that I
will return to on occasion.

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:18.878
So again,
you have this mass on this spring.

00:09:18.878 --> 00:09:21.485
Here's some of my work going way back.

00:09:21.485 --> 00:09:23.364
This is a grating light valve.

00:09:23.364 --> 00:09:25.324
These are ribbons that move up and

00:09:25.324 --> 00:09:29.525
down to create diffraction patterns
that we can use to modulate light,

00:09:29.525 --> 00:09:33.390
and we can also use them as sensors
in different configurations.

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.810
This is again, so
these ribbons have a mass.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.850
They have a spring constant to
carry masses on the spring.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:47.760
So I think I'm trying to make the argument
now that many sensors can be shown,

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.980
or can be through described in models,
as a mass on a spring.

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:58.687
So now I'm going to show you a little
bit about how that actually merge.

00:09:58.687 --> 00:10:02.817
This is to give you
a little bit of a feel for

00:10:02.817 --> 00:10:08.490
how these type of sensors work,
and also how they scale.

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:09.964
That's going to be my main point.

00:10:09.964 --> 00:10:14.014
So here we see this is a very
simple spring board that I

00:10:14.014 --> 00:10:16.518
put together here on a.

00:10:16.518 --> 00:10:19.077
This is actually a hacksaw blade.

00:10:19.077 --> 00:10:23.936
If I engage it like that, you can see
that it's a broad based at the frequency.

00:10:23.936 --> 00:10:25.338
That's the resting frequency.

00:10:25.338 --> 00:10:30.124
The resting frequency is the frequency
where you have maximum energy storage in

00:10:30.124 --> 00:10:32.704
terms of mechanical energy in the system.

00:10:32.704 --> 00:10:37.303
Now it moves pretty fast here now,
but it is already a sensory.

00:10:37.303 --> 00:10:42.035
If I think of this as a sensor, right now
of course it's actually an accelerometer.

00:10:42.035 --> 00:10:46.990
It sits in a accelerating field and
it's bent a little bit.

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:50.671
You can't really see it, but
it's very much standing there,

00:10:50.671 --> 00:10:55.640
and that's part of the problem that you
need to be able to measure how far bends.

00:10:55.640 --> 00:11:00.200
If I wanted to use it as a magnetic
sensor instead, which I could do,

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:04.933
I could bring this magnet in here and
you can see that it starts to bend.

00:11:04.933 --> 00:11:08.377
You're using, oop there is snapped,
and now I actually

00:11:08.377 --> 00:11:13.210
have a better accelerometer than I had
before at least, it's more sensitive.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:16.000
Now you can more easily see that it bends.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.805
Now, even with just eyeballing
this you can see that there

00:11:20.805 --> 00:11:24.070
is an accelerating field this way.

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:25.170
That's why,

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:29.020
if it was bent that way instead you would
know that I had turned it upside down.

00:11:29.020 --> 00:11:30.520
Take a look at this.

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:35.794
When I now engage it, I think that if you
remember back to when I did it before,

00:11:35.794 --> 00:11:38.810
it moves quite a bit
faster than it does now.

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:43.083
So there's some things that happened
to the statistics here when I make

00:11:43.083 --> 00:11:44.301
it more sensitive.

00:11:44.301 --> 00:11:49.131
I made it more sensitive to
gravitational fields to just as a scale,

00:11:49.131 --> 00:11:52.265
if you like,
I made a more sensitive scale or

00:11:52.265 --> 00:11:57.459
more sensitive accelerometer by doing
this, but I also made it slower.

00:11:57.459 --> 00:12:00.011
So that's the point that I want to make.

00:12:00.011 --> 00:12:05.300
If you now switch back to my slides
here then we can see that what

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:12.290
I've showed you is essentially the system
that I've shown here in the figure.

00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:15.670
There is a spring,
that's the spring constant there k.

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:20.800
There's a mass that's moving on it's,
I didn't talk about this, but there's some

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:24.280
damping, but you can certainly see that
the swinging didn't go on forever.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:28.351
So damped out,
maybe they didn't damp out fast enough.

00:12:28.351 --> 00:12:30.035
I'm looking at it over here.

00:12:30.035 --> 00:12:33.472
Now, it's still vibrating, but
it is, the vibration goes down,

00:12:33.472 --> 00:12:34.919
so there's the damper, and

00:12:34.919 --> 00:12:38.620
then there is something that we
didn't really have in our experiment.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:42.931
I asked you to just eyeball
how much the thing moved, and

00:12:42.931 --> 00:12:48.820
that's some kind of measurement for
how much a mass is actually displaced.

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:53.135
So if I think of this as an accelerometer,
if I modeled an accelerometer this way,

00:12:53.135 --> 00:12:56.900
then I have a proof mass m that is
going to be displaced by acceleration.

00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:00.398
If I accelerate the whole system and

00:13:00.398 --> 00:13:05.876
get a displacement of m going to,
that gives me a force,

00:13:05.876 --> 00:13:10.785
m times a, and
that has to equal the spring force.

00:13:10.785 --> 00:13:12.334
So I can calculate.

00:13:12.334 --> 00:13:17.610
Once I just measure this displacement,
I can calculate the acceleration

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:22.374
that the mass has been subject to
by knowing the spring constants,

00:13:22.374 --> 00:13:26.480
measuring the displacement,
and dividing by the mass.

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:28.540
That's my measurement, and
this is how accelerometers work.

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:32.620
This is how they work in your phone,
this is how they work in your car,

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:39.560
this is the exact mechanism that is being
used when an airbag deploys in your car.

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:46.185
So you have to sort of either live with
more and just the simple formula that

00:13:46.185 --> 00:13:50.295
tells us what the acceleration is
if you have a certain displacement.

00:13:50.295 --> 00:13:55.175
You also have to realize that there
is a bandwidth associated with that,

00:13:55.175 --> 00:13:59.735
we have showed you that,
that once I made the displacement larger,

00:13:59.735 --> 00:14:04.540
by putting more mass on my spring,
I had a slower resonance frequency.

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:09.880
That's essentially meaning that I can't
measure signals that are fast anymore,

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:14.544
I can only measure slow signals once
I do that, so that's the tradeoff.

00:14:14.544 --> 00:14:21.005
The tradeoff is shown here.

00:14:21.005 --> 00:14:22.690
Well actually I should
tell you one thing first.

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:27.800
What I asked you to do when we
looked at the video was to just

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:32.780
eyeball the displacement in
a MEMS sensor we don't do that.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:38.529
We use some kind of adaptor
characteristics to measure displacement,

00:14:38.529 --> 00:14:43.432
and in our accelerometer,
we use capacitive measurement.

00:14:43.432 --> 00:14:47.210
So now you see that I have the same system
I had before, but I added a little bit.

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:52.940
I added a little circuit here that just
basically puts voltage on these plates,

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:54.840
across these three plates.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:59.670
So there's a counter electrode down here,
the mass has become an electrode, and

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:01.494
I have an electric field here.

00:15:01.494 --> 00:15:04.901
I can now measure the distance
between those two plates by looking at

00:15:04.901 --> 00:15:08.870
the capacitance in this circuit,
the capacitance between them.

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:10.321
That's a very, very good measurement.

00:15:10.321 --> 00:15:13.880
I can measure very,
very accurate in this way.

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:16.880
I can measure distances that
are much more than the size

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:19.500
of an atom with this type of technique.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:24.790
So, and this is part of my sort of
small is beautiful argument, that can

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:28.606
really match a very small displacement,
very accurately with this technique.

00:15:28.606 --> 00:15:34.970
If I wanted to measure something
that's large, and moves a large rate,

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:41.349
for instance your car is pulling up to
a to a stop sign or a traffic device.

00:15:41.349 --> 00:15:43.841
And then after a little
while it's changed,

00:15:43.841 --> 00:15:47.286
then it's measuring the position
of your car magnetically.

00:15:47.286 --> 00:15:49.790
That's too large distance, and so for

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:54.060
small distances very accurate
measurements you use electric field.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:16:00.246
So once you have that,
you have the measurement of precision,

00:16:00.246 --> 00:16:05.710
delta z, then you can calculate
the acceleration, like I said.

00:16:05.710 --> 00:16:08.820
And I can rewrite that in terms of
that formula that I showed you for

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:11.170
the resonance frequency In this way.

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:17.310
So here, this is really the central
design equation for an accelerometer.

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:20.091
And of course, like I said,
it's not only the accelerometer,

00:16:20.091 --> 00:16:22.317
it's always the other sensors and
actuators too.

00:16:22.317 --> 00:16:26.510
So unless you wanted to not measure
the smallest possible acceleration in

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:28.236
the given bandwidth.

00:16:28.236 --> 00:16:31.335
So you want to go up to certain frequency,
there's certain signals that are so

00:16:31.335 --> 00:16:36.720
fast you don't care, but they do care
about certain signals up to a frequency f.

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:40.250
You could try to make that happen by

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:44.180
having very low [INAUDIBLE] and
the very large mass.

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:49.220
And I would get a strong signal but I also
would get a very low resonance frequency.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:57.520
And if you take a look at that frequency,
resonance frequency,

00:16:57.520 --> 00:17:03.847
that's [INAUDIBLE] the square
[INAUDIBLE] over the mass.

00:17:03.847 --> 00:17:06.888
So that attempt isn't very good.

00:17:06.888 --> 00:17:12.576
Making a good sensor by making
a very weak spring and a large mass.

00:17:12.576 --> 00:17:15.300
Well, it sort of works but
it doesn't really work for

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:16.980
the frequency side we're interested in.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:20.770
What we can try to do is to make
a miniaturized accelerometer.

00:17:20.770 --> 00:17:25.460
So now we have a very small mass, but
we also then compensate by making a very,

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:26.623
very weak spring.

00:17:26.623 --> 00:17:30.440
It's only really the ratio
to that is important.

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:31.540
So small is okay.

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:35.370
It's okay to have a small mass, because
you can also make it a weak spring and

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:41.610
it still remains in a good,
small, measurable acceleration.

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:45.858
So what it really comes down to is
[INAUDIBLE] is displacement [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:45.858 --> 00:17:47.350
very accurately.

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:51.340
[INAUDIBLE], that's nothing to be good
enough using capacitors if you measure

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:52.919
that very, very accurately.

00:17:52.919 --> 00:17:57.775
But the key there is to make them so
we [INAUDIBLE] small structures,

00:17:57.775 --> 00:18:01.690
because they have excellent
position sensitivity.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:07.950
And you can just try any resonance
frequency you want, because it's a ratio

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:14.815
of two different quantities of the spring
cross in the mass that matters.

00:18:14.815 --> 00:18:18.935
These lead us to matter of main
argument over these last few slides.

00:18:18.935 --> 00:18:22.715
Anyone who's used miniaturized sensors and
you want to make

00:18:22.715 --> 00:18:26.490
them as small as you can possibly make
them and that's where MEMS comes in.

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:31.732
So MEMS stands for microelectromechanical
systems and like I've said before,

00:18:31.732 --> 00:18:37.070
these are systems or sensors that are made
using integrated circuit technology.

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:44.060
This is something that started way back
and Stanford was very central to it.

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:47.939
This is some figures from
an article I read in 1983,

00:18:47.939 --> 00:18:51.300
when it was published
in Scientific American.

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:55.820
And James Angell and
the other gentlemen on this

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:59.940
paper were here at Stanford at the time
and they did some very, very cool things.

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:02.840
They wrote about this gas chromatograph,

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.400
which is this,
I’m going to explain how that works.

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:10.109
This actually is not an example of
something that's a mass in the spring.

00:19:10.109 --> 00:19:14.598
It's a wafer scale integration,
it basically

00:19:14.598 --> 00:19:20.210
measure the composition of gases,
the gas mixtures.

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:25.536
This one here in the lower right-hand
corner is an accelerometer, then of course

00:19:25.536 --> 00:19:30.415
here, you can immediately recognize
the string right there and the mass.

00:19:30.415 --> 00:19:32.605
So I'll know this is
a spring with a mass on it.

00:19:32.605 --> 00:19:35.665
So this is exactly what I just showed you.

00:19:35.665 --> 00:19:37.245
And then here's another one.

00:19:37.245 --> 00:19:40.442
This is a pressure sensor.

00:19:40.442 --> 00:19:46.710
If you're measuring just static pressure
if you just try to follow the weather or

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:52.440
something, then maybe the [INAUDIBLE]
frequency isn't that important.

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:54.720
But if you use something like this
as a microphone, [INAUDIBLE] and

00:19:54.720 --> 00:20:00.620
many of you have these kinds of
microphones in your cellphones.

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:03.923
Then again, you have your resonance
frequency as the key parameter and

00:20:03.923 --> 00:20:05.180
you have the same.

00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:09.920
Sort of you have the same argument for
making this as small as possible.

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:13.030
This was an incredibly
powerful article for me.

00:20:13.030 --> 00:20:16.330
This is where I did what I did later.

00:20:16.330 --> 00:20:19.421
Three years after this came out,
I was a staffer myself.

00:20:19.421 --> 00:20:22.390
So and I lived in Norway when I read it.

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:25.950
So this was sort of life altering for me.

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:31.300
Another thing that came out was
article by Kurt E Peterson.

00:20:32.652 --> 00:20:36.460
In the particular figure that
I pulled out of that article.

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:42.290
He had article was or the publication was
called Silicon as a Mechanical Material.

00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:46.355
And of course, that goes to the heart of
what I'm arguing to, silicon is really it.

00:20:46.355 --> 00:20:49.390
That is the best possible
material to make sensors out of.

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:54.180
He solved, and
I'm sort of reproducing that figure here,

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:56.920
a piece of work that was done by
a previous student here at Stanford.

00:20:56.920 --> 00:21:03.000
Roylance was her name, and she made
some of the first MEMS accelerometers.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:08.110
So these are,
basically they're forefathers if you like

00:21:08.110 --> 00:21:11.920
of the accelerometers that you have in
your cellphone and in your car right now.

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:14.260
This structure as you see there and
[INAUDIBLE] recognize.

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:17.610
If you look at this structure,
that you have a spring and approved mass,

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:20.130
exactly the same thing as
I've been talking about.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:26.628
So, when we make MEMS, when we make
microelectricalmechanical systems,

00:21:26.628 --> 00:21:28.390
we use many materials.

00:21:28.390 --> 00:21:32.740
Essentially if you go through
the periodic table of elements,

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:35.890
almost all of them have been
used in some fashion, either for

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:39.230
fabricating MEMS or
being part of MEMS structures.

00:21:39.230 --> 00:21:40.698
But then really the most important parts,

00:21:40.698 --> 00:21:42.138
I'm not going to go
through the whole list.

00:21:42.138 --> 00:21:46.841
We have a whole large number of
materials that we like to use and

00:21:46.841 --> 00:21:48.654
that'd be okay to use.

00:21:48.654 --> 00:21:56.000
The silicon is the sort of mainstay,
the one we use always.

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:58.500
And the reason for
that is that's it's stronger then steel,

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:00.970
lighter than aluminum,
and it's single crystal.

00:22:00.970 --> 00:22:03.370
Then, it can also be used
in polycrystalline form,

00:22:03.370 --> 00:22:08.100
a single crystal in particular,
some very, very good properties.

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:12.970
And there's no other mechanical material
out there that's anywhere like it.

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:16.852
If you think about construction and so
forth, you think about steel maybe.

00:22:16.852 --> 00:22:21.672
And steel can be single crystal,
copper can be a single crystal,

00:22:21.672 --> 00:22:26.240
but they never are when you
are talking about constructions.

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:31.370
Only silicon is the single
crystalline in mechanical systems,

00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:33.930
and that's what makes it so unique.

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:35.400
So just a little bit about silicon,

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:40.060
it's in the group four in
the periodic table of elements.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:44.080
And therefore, it has the diamond
like structure, same as carbon and

00:22:44.080 --> 00:22:45.960
it crystallizes.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:49.720
That means that there are some
certain planes within silicon

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:51.370
that are more robust than others.

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:54.717
So if you expose it through
chemical [INAUDIBLE],

00:22:54.717 --> 00:22:58.770
some planes just won't
etch as well as others.

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:02.355
And that means that you can
make structures like these.

00:23:02.355 --> 00:23:03.890
I'm showing you some structures here.

00:23:03.890 --> 00:23:07.759
There you can make very
well-defined structures that

00:23:07.759 --> 00:23:11.470
are essentially terminating
on the 11 1 planes.

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:14.690
These [INAUDIBLE] planes are more
robust than the other planes.

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:19.000
And what that allows you to do is to
machine very accurately in parallel, and

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:20.320
that's the key point.

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:21.770
You can make machine in parallel.

00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:25.720
To think about a machine shop,
you have a machine there,

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:28.220
you take a piece of aluminum or
a piece of steel, and you machine it.

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:30.233
You do it at one at a time.

00:23:30.233 --> 00:23:38.310
If you go back to that, that's
a visual light processing technology.

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:43.040
These instruments they have a million
little machines on their chip.

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:45.120
You couldn't possibly
make them one by one.

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:47.900
So this idea of making
them all in parallel and

00:23:47.900 --> 00:23:51.130
making them by chemistry
as opposed to by machining.

00:23:51.130 --> 00:23:54.230
Machining by chemistry if you like,
that's very central and

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:55.280
here's how you start that.

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:59.410
And here are some of
those typical sensors.

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:04.420
One thing that was very popular for
a long time was to use piezoresistive

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:08.250
sensors to do the measurement
of displacement.

00:24:08.250 --> 00:24:12.690
Actually, capacity before,
this is again from that same paper that

00:24:12.690 --> 00:24:16.458
Where the pressure sensor in
the lower right here is from

00:24:16.458 --> 00:24:21.260
that Scientific American article
by Anschiel and his coworkers.

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:26.160
So this is one example of how you make
a pressure sensor that is actually not

00:24:26.160 --> 00:24:30.848
capacitive in this particular case but
uses another transduction mechanism.

00:24:30.848 --> 00:24:36.530
One thing that made a huge Impact on the
silicon valley was using these things for

00:24:36.530 --> 00:24:42.900
inkjet nozzles, so this is the basis of
that transformation that happened within

00:24:42.900 --> 00:24:48.300
HP, Hewlett Packard, when they became sort
of a printer company for a few years.

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:52.560
This is a part of an inkjet printer.

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:57.140
Of course that was something that's
maybe has come and gone but it was very,

00:24:57.140 --> 00:25:00.180
very important for a long time.

00:25:00.180 --> 00:25:03.091
So this is by machining
things out of silicon, so

00:25:03.091 --> 00:25:05.680
out of material that I think so highly of.

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:10.519
Another way of making micromachines
is to deposit layers on

00:25:10.519 --> 00:25:13.430
top of silicon and then shape that.

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:16.476
And this is the sort of
canonical example of that.

00:25:16.476 --> 00:25:20.520
This is a Combdrive that could be sitting
in something like a timing circuit or

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:25.640
an exometer, and in fact, that particular

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:30.970
technology has been taken
to extremes by a number

00:25:30.970 --> 00:25:35.826
of companies and also institutions
around the United States.

00:25:35.826 --> 00:25:40.550
So Sandia in particular made a very,
very sophisticated

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:44.360
surface micromachining process that
you can make almost anything in.

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:46.440
So and

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:51.240
then of course surface micromachining
is also the underlying technology for

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:56.500
that digital light processing technology
that I already talked about a little bit.

00:25:57.610 --> 00:26:02.730
Another aspect of fabrication that
sort of leads up to modern day

00:26:02.730 --> 00:26:06.500
micromachining in MEMS is
deep reactive ion etching.

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:11.180
Some of the most seminal work
in deep reactive ion etching for

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:13.680
MEMS is also done here at Stanford.

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:18.515
How it works is that when you try to etch
into silicon you tend to sort of get

00:26:18.515 --> 00:26:20.913
shapes that are not exactly ideal.

00:26:20.913 --> 00:26:25.188
So if you take a look at that cross
section in the upper left hand corner,

00:26:25.188 --> 00:26:30.270
I started to etch into silicon, I'd get
these slopes that were rounded side walls.

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:34.219
And what I could do then, and what
happens automatically in this process,

00:26:34.219 --> 00:26:37.880
in deep reactive ion etching,
is that I deposit a polymer.

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:41.000
And then I etched a polymer
at the bottom of that hole.

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:46.380
I executed a directional etch so
that the bottom is exposed and

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:48.448
I can repeat the process.

00:26:48.448 --> 00:26:52.680
And I get, instead of sort of getting
a very large or rounded sidewall,

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:57.480
I get a sidewall that consists of
these scallops, little scallops, and

00:26:57.480 --> 00:26:59.130
they can be very, very small.

00:26:59.130 --> 00:27:00.830
So essentially, even, I've done this.

00:27:00.830 --> 00:27:04.995
I keep repeating this,
this deposit polymer then etch,

00:27:04.995 --> 00:27:10.288
deposit polymer then etch process,
I get something like what I show here

00:27:10.288 --> 00:27:16.400
in the bottom at right hand corner where
I basically get straight side walls.

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:18.710
This was a huge first step forward,

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:20.830
because now we can shape
things differently.

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:25.510
Now you were no long dependent on the
crystalline orientation of our silicon.

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:29.970
Now we can shape it more arbitrarily, so
you can do things like I'm showing here.

00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:33.898
This is a very much used
type of vapor in maps,

00:27:33.898 --> 00:27:39.090
it's silicon insulator, what it means
is I have taken a silicon wafer,

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:43.934
deposited oxide on it and then put a layer
of silicon on top of that oxide again, so

00:27:43.934 --> 00:27:45.610
that's where I'm showing on the top here.

00:27:45.610 --> 00:27:48.220
And then we just take that DRIE and

00:27:48.220 --> 00:27:51.280
just shape that top layer
into whatever we want.

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:53.840
And then we remove from oxide underneath,

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:58.550
from where we want to let them
have free stranding structures.

00:27:58.550 --> 00:28:05.060
This is essentially the basis process for
a large number of MEMS devices.

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:06.640
And here is sort of just one example.

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:12.543
This is close to 20 years old now, so it's
not sort of the modern type necessarily.

00:28:12.543 --> 00:28:15.030
And there is a couple of
points I wanted to make here.

00:28:15.030 --> 00:28:16.490
It's a very simple process.

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:23.390
Therefore the structure that you see
is made in one mask in an SOI wafer,

00:28:23.390 --> 00:28:28.220
and it has a very,
very powerful functionality.

00:28:28.220 --> 00:28:30.610
It's actually a fiber optic switch,
this particular example.

00:28:30.610 --> 00:28:34.270
The point I want to make
is that it's way too large.

00:28:34.270 --> 00:28:40.927
I said that's MEMS One of
the fundamental reasons to use MEMS,

00:28:40.927 --> 00:28:44.135
and particularly in the Internet of
Things is that you're going to have so

00:28:44.135 --> 00:28:46.232
many sensors, and
that they are super small.

00:28:47.410 --> 00:28:52.808
So, the question is how do we take this
type of technology I talked about there,

00:28:52.808 --> 00:28:57.645
the chemical machining,
the surface micromachining, and the DRIE,

00:28:57.645 --> 00:29:01.290
how do we combine those to
make really small sensors?

00:29:01.290 --> 00:29:06.710
That I think is where the futures are,
very, very small sensors.

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:11.588
And here's one example, this is from my
own work where we've been able to put

00:29:11.588 --> 00:29:15.117
a large number of the third
functionalities together,

00:29:15.117 --> 00:29:17.894
this is an atomic force
microscope that is so

00:29:17.894 --> 00:29:21.142
small that it fits on
the facet of an optical fiber.

00:29:21.142 --> 00:29:24.280
A standard optical fiber is 125 microns,
so

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:28.679
that's a little bit more than
a tenth of a millimeter in diameter.

00:29:28.679 --> 00:29:34.677
And the output, a fully functional
atomic force microscope on top of it.

00:29:34.677 --> 00:29:38.690
So that is sort of a tour de
force in fabrication if you like.

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:44.370
But it also shows that we now have
the technologies to go smaller.

00:29:44.370 --> 00:29:49.740
One of the key technologies behind that
particular development is shown here,

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:54.860
that's Hydrogen Annealing, so If you
start out with a piece of silicon and

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:58.750
you print at low pressure
in a hydrogen ambient, and

00:29:58.750 --> 00:30:02.820
heat it up a little bit,
you'll actually get sort of

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:05.750
a reconformation of the silicon,
it will flow.

00:30:05.750 --> 00:30:09.380
It still stay in sort of
a single crystalline form but

00:30:09.380 --> 00:30:12.300
it can make all kinds of very
very interesting devices.

00:30:12.300 --> 00:30:17.560
What I'm showing here is on
the left of a photonic crystal,

00:30:17.560 --> 00:30:22.000
on the right I'm showing that how we
can actually use this to make boards,

00:30:22.000 --> 00:30:25.570
I'm going to later in a little bit
more careful detail later, one of in

00:30:25.570 --> 00:30:32.010
the picture in the lower left hand side,
no sorry, lower right hand side.

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:35.932
It's one of my favorites because you
can actually see in those boards that

00:30:35.932 --> 00:30:39.342
are created you can see
the crystalline facets of the silicon.

00:30:39.342 --> 00:30:44.230
So we are showing that this is
in fact sort of a crystalline

00:30:44.230 --> 00:30:48.970
material and even that these
boards show this very clearly.

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:56.360
So just to show you,
this is what I think is the basis for

00:30:56.360 --> 00:31:00.875
a number of sort of very important
MEMS sensors going forward,

00:31:00.875 --> 00:31:03.900
sort of assess a technology
that allows you to make more or

00:31:03.900 --> 00:31:07.270
less arbitrary shapes of silicon and
I'm demonstrating that here.

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:12.752
So we start here with a, following
it numerically from one and then up.

00:31:12.752 --> 00:31:16.668
There's a piece of silicon,
we deposit a matching layer,

00:31:16.668 --> 00:31:20.598
we pattern something into
the non-matching level three.

00:31:20.598 --> 00:31:23.921
And then we etch into the silicon.

00:31:23.921 --> 00:31:26.290
So only one match so far, and
in fact there can only be one match.

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:29.242
And then I do a little
trick there in number five,

00:31:29.242 --> 00:31:32.398
I cover everything
conformally with a thin cell.

00:31:32.398 --> 00:31:37.450
And then in six, I've continued my trick,
I've now removed things from the bottom.

00:31:37.450 --> 00:31:40.409
That's just a sort of an idea
we got from the DRIE, right,

00:31:40.409 --> 00:31:44.080
I remove things from,
for example, surfaces.

00:31:44.080 --> 00:31:46.226
The top surface doesn't matter,

